  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "When \\(y^2+my+2\\) is divided by \\(y-1\\) the quotient is \\(f(y)\\) and the remainder is \\(R_1.\\) When \\(y^2+my+2\\) is divided by \\(y+1\\) the quotient is \\(g(y)\\) and the remainder is \\(R_2.\\) If \\(R_1=R_2,\\) then \\(m\\) is:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(0\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(1\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(-1\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "an undetermined constant"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "a",
    "value": "0"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1965-p27.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "The remainder theorem gives \\(R_1=1+m+2=m+3\\) and \\(R_2=1-m+2=3-m.\\) Equality implies \\(m=0.\\)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A.",
      "hints": [
        "Use the remainder theorem at \\(y=1\\) and \\(y=-1\\)",
        "Set \\(m+3=3-m\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
